memcpy, memmove, memset, strlen, memcmp, memchr, strchr, strchrnul,
strncmp and strcpy for x86-64 that work sixteen bytes at a time with
SSE2, which every x86-64 processor has, and strcmp as mlibc's own SSE2
version. The portable versions look at one byte at a time (strlen,
memcmp, memchr, strchr, strchrnul, strncmp, strcpy, and memmove when it
copies downwards) or move eight (memcpy, memset). mlibc chooses among
its SSE2, SSE4.2 and AVX2 strcmp with an ifunc, which the Radix
toolchain does not build ("C++ compiler supports ifunc: NO"), so strcmp
was the byte loop as well. In PostgreSQL's select-only pgbench these
functions were 13% of the instructions in user mode.

A function that reads a string of unknown length loads 16 aligned
bytes, which never cross a page, and masks off the bytes before the
string. One that compares two strings loads 16 bytes unaligned only
where both have 16 left in their page, and goes a byte at a time to the
end of the page otherwise. Copies of up to 64 bytes read every byte
into registers before writing any; longer ones read their first and
last pieces, copy 64 bytes a loop to aligned stores, upwards or
downwards by which way the two overlap, then write the pieces. memcpy is
memmove. memcmp returns the difference of the first bytes that differ,
as glibc does, where the portable version returned -1 or 1.

options/ansi/x86_64/essential.cpp has memcpy, memmove, memset and
strlen, built into the dynamic linker too as essential.cpp is, and
options/ansi/x86_64/string.cpp the rest; MLIBC_ARCH_HAS_STRING_FUNCTIONS
leaves out the portable versions.

